   "source": "## 8. Interpretation to write after execution\n\n    Use actual saved measurements to answer:\n\n    - At which epoch was the best checkpoint selected? Do training and validation curves suggest overfitting or incomplete learning?\n    - Which digits are confused most often? Use the confusion matrix and per-class report.\n    - How do accuracy, macro F1 and computational cost compare with the other measured runs?\n    - What can this single run support, and which conclusions would require repeated runs or a more controlled budget?\n\n    **Personal synthesis:** explain what you learned in your own words. This cannot honestly be completed before you run and inspect the experiments.\n\n    ## Sources\n\n    - Lab specification: `Atelier2.pdf`, supplied by the student.\n    - Student's earlier project: `atelier2-mnist-main.zip`, used as a reference.\n    - [PyTorch documentation](https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/index.html).\n    - [Torchvision model documentation](https://docs.pytorch.org/vision/stable/models.html).\n    - For ViT: [Brian Pulfer's tutorial specified in the lab](https://medium.com/@brianpulfer/vision-transformers-from-scratch-pytorch-a-step-by-step-guide-96c3313c2e0c). Our vectorized attention uses full-token Q/K/V projections; the architecture and training budget are our agreed variant.\n",
